"""
A request to the Hiawatha in front to ban the client of this request for
some seconds: the header X-Hiawatha-Ban on the answer.  Hiawatha reads it
only where its host says BanByCGI = yes, cuts the seconds to the second
value of that line when there is one, removes the header before the answer
leaves, and from then on drops every connection of the client until the
ban ends.  It logs the ban in its system log.

The client is what Hiawatha names in REMOTE_ADDR: behind a CDN or another
proxy only HideProxy makes that the client and not the proxy, and an
address the BanlistMask allows is never banned.  Without BanByCGI the
header is a string nobody reads; nothing in the answer tells.

What earns a ban is the application's: a filled honeypot, or a limit of
Wolly.Limit run over many times.
"""
import Wolly.Response (
    Response
);

module Wolly.Ban is
    """
    The longest ban withBan asks for: 86400 seconds, a day.
    """
    function longestBanSeconds(): Int64
        ensures result = 86400;

    """
    The response with the header X-Hiawatha-Ban: seconds, cut to
    longestBanSeconds.
    """
    function withBan(response: Response, seconds: Int64): Response
        requires seconds > 0;
end module.
